      "id": "card.ancient_rome.073.c1",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "Some Roman harbor walls have stood in seawater for two thousand years."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.073.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Ash and lime",
      "body": "Roman concrete mixed lime with volcanic ash, called pozzolana, and chunks of rock or brick. Pozzolana is named after Pozzuoli, near Naples, where the ash was dug. Concrete made with it could set even underwater, which let Romans build harbors in the sea.",
      "callout": "Pozzolana: volcanic ash from near Naples"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.073.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "New shapes",
      "body": "Concrete let Romans build arches, vaults and domes on a scale never seen before. Walls were usually faced with brick or stone, so the concrete core was hidden from view."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.073.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "Roman concrete could repair itself.",
      "context": "A 2023 study found that lumps of lime in the mix could react with water seeping into cracks and seal them."
    },
    {
